[Black "Chapter Summary: Key Takeaways"]
[Result "*"]

 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Outpost: A square on the fourth to seventh rank, which is protected by a pawn and not attackable by any of the opponent’s pawns. (2) General guidelines: Outposts are valuable for much the same reason as weak squares. These include being central, advanced, easy to reach, and close to the action. (3) Outposts vs weak squares: While an outpost should generally be preferred, many times a weak square will be the more attractive target, so it's important to remain flexible. (4) Minor pieces on outposts: An advanced outposted piece tends to have a paralyzing effect regardless of whether it is in the centre, kingside or queenside. Kingside pieces often lead to devastating attacks, while queenside pieces are often used to guide pawns to promotion. Central pieces are flexible and can pivot to either flank when needed. (5) Maneuvering to outposts: The ability to quickly identify typical maneuvers is very helpful, the most common of which is a knight maneuver. These can range from short (2-move maneuvers) to longer 5 or 6-move maneuvers. In the case of slower plans, it’s important to check if your opponent has active counterplay that can spoil your plans. (6) Creating outposts: Outposts are created through a change in the structure. Sometimes the errors are unforced, while other times you will have to actively change the pawn formation by advancing a pawn, executing a pawn break, or exchanging a set of pawns. (7) The fight for outposts: When fighting for an outpost, you will often need to seek out favorable piece exchanges to enhance the strength of your plan. When fighting against outposts, it is important to stay on guard, as you may have to anticipate your opponent’s intention and play prophylactic moves to stop them from carrying out their plan. (8) Power outposts: Most outposts are normal outposts, but if you control a given square with more than one pawn, then such a square becomes a ‘power outpost’. These tend to be particularly strong and are often game-ending positional assets. (9) Pseudo outposts: While not technically an outpost, depending on the rest of the board conditions such squares can function like a regular outpost, in which case the usual rules will apply. } 1. { -KEY- }  
 *

