 { Find the best sequence for White. Tip: be careful to find the most precise move at every turn. } 14. { -KEY- } Nd6+ $1 Bxd6 15. exd6 Qxd6 16. h5 $1 ( 16. Bxg7 $2 { does not give White any real advantage after, e.g., } Rg8 17. Bf6 Nb4 { when suddenly Black has a lot of pressure against White's kingside. } ) 
Nge7 17. Bxg7 Rg8 18. h6 $16 { Correct! This was a very tricky example as White had a narrow path to an advantage and had to combine positional and dynamic play to find the best moves. The sequence involved three steps: 1. Use the outpost to gain the bishop pair and open up the a1-h8 diagonal. 2. Play the in-between move h4-h5 to prepare capturing on g7. 3. After capturing on g7, keep the g-file shut down by defending the bishop with h5-h6. } ( 18. Bb2 $4 { would even lose the game! For example, } Rxg3 19. fxg3 Qxg3 20. Kf1 Nd4 21. Bxd4 Bxf3 $19 { White must give up their queen in order to avoid being mated on the next move. This brings the puzzle set to a close. Congratulations on finishing the chapter in full, and I will see you in the next one! } ) 

